  "limitations": [
    "It was not possible to find a microphone site further from the carriageway due to factory premises (p. 4).",
    "It was not possible to measure at a greater distance from the barrier due to the narrowing of the road and the possible obstruction to vehicles visiting the nearest properties (p. 5).",
    "Speeds were only measured in the eastbound lanes but it was assumed for estimation purposes that speeds were similar in the westbound lanes (p. 11).",
    "The assumption of vehicle width and angle of incidence in the model slightly underestimates the degree of screening of reflected sound (p. 12).",
    "The effects of vehicle speed on source strength were not taken into account in the model, considered to have only a small effect (p. 12).",
    "The simplified geometry assumed in the prediction model, including safety fences, reflective ground beams, and limited barrier length/curvature, may have reduced the measured noise level increases (p. 14).",
    "It is possible that the sides of many large vehicles do not provide conditions for specular reflections, which could explain the failure to observe significant changes in LA1 (p. 15).",
    "The less than perfect absorption and reflective properties of the barrier panel faces in real-world conditions may have slightly reduced the size of noise level increases (p. 15).",
    "Further carefully controlled field studies are required to fully quantify the effects of vehicle flow in scattering sound and site parameters such as barrier separation to height ratio (p. 18)."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical field study investigated the effects of changing noise barrier face material from sound absorptive to reflective on roadside noise levels at two sites (motorway and dual carriageway). The primary finding was that noise levels generally increased by less than 1 dB when the barrier face became reflective, with the maximum increase of 2.09 dB LA10 observed at a high receiver position (7.5m) 15m behind parallel barriers. The study found that UK traffic noise prediction models (CRTN) tended to overestimate the noise increase from reflective barriers, with measured changes being generally smaller than predicted. Limitations include the complexity of real-world traffic scattering and air turbulence, simplified geometries in prediction models, and the inherent variability of field measurements."
